  "card_variant": "random",
  "short_desc": "Cryptography protects confidentiality, integrity and authenticity. It is behind HTTPS, password storage, disk encryption, software signing and messaging.",
  "lead_author": {
   "name": "RedKross Academy Faculty",
   "title": "Applied Cryptography",
   "role": "RedKross Research Foundation",
   "avatar": "/assets/crest-clean-circle.png"
  },
  "overview": "Cryptography protects confidentiality, integrity and authenticity. It is behind HTTPS, password storage, disk encryption, software signing and messaging. Most real-world failures come not from broken algorithms but from choosing the wrong primitive, generating keys badly, reusing nonces, mishandling certificates or misconfiguring protocols. This course teaches applied cryptography for engineers and defenders: - security goals, randomness and threat models; - symmetric encryption and authenticated encryption; - public-key cryptography and key exchange; - hashing, MACs and password hashing; - digital signatures, certificates and PKI; - TLS and its configuration; - implementation pitfalls and library selection. It ends with building and testing a file encryption utility. The emphasis is on using well-reviewed libraries correctly, not on inventing algorithms.",
  "why_it_matters": [
   "Real failures examined in this course include: - a predictable random number generator that compromised keys across Debian systems (2008); - ECB-mode encryption in a popular video service (2020); - weak RSA key generation in hundreds of thousands of national ID cards (2017); - a certificate authority whose compromise led browsers to remove trust (2011); - a reused signature nonce that exposed a console maker's signing key (2010). Standards are moving too. NIST approved its first post-quantum cryptography standards (FIPS 203-205) in August 2024. The IETF published RFC 9846, an updated TLS 1.3 specification that forbids TLS 1.0 and 1.1. Public TLS certificate lifetimes are being cut in steps towards 47 days by 2029."
  ],
  "intended_audience": [
   "Software and security engineers, system administrators managing TLS and keys, security analysts and auditors reviewing cryptographic controls, and learners who completed Courses 01, 03 and 04."
  ],
  "prerequisites": [
   "Course 01",
   "Course 02 (TCP, TLS basics)",
   "Course 04 (Python). Comfort with binary and hexadecimal. No advanced mathematics is required",
   "concepts are explained intuitively"
  ],
  "required_software_hardware": [
   "Python 3.10+ with the cryptography package (pip install cryptography); the OpenSSL 3.x command line (included with Git for Windows, macOS Homebrew or Linux distributions); 4 GB RAM."
  ],
  "what_you_will_learn": [
   "State cryptographic security goals, generate randomness correctly and model threats to keys and data.",
   "Select and use authenticated encryption and manage symmetric keys.",
   "Explain and use RSA, elliptic-curve cryptography and key exchange, and the post-quantum transition.",
   "Use hash functions, HMAC and password hashing with correct parameters.",
   "Create and validate signatures and certificate chains, and operate a PKI.",
   "Explain the TLS 1.3 handshake and configure and test TLS securely.",
   "Recognise and prevent implementation misuse, and select appropriate libraries.",
   "Build, test and document a secure file encryption and integrity tool."
  ],
  "learning_objectives": [
   "LO1: State cryptographic security goals, generate randomness correctly and model threats to keys and data.",
   "LO2: Select and use authenticated encryption and manage symmetric keys.",
   "LO3: Explain and use RSA, elliptic-curve cryptography and key exchange, and the post-quantum transition.",
   "LO4: Use hash functions, HMAC and password hashing with correct parameters.",
   "LO5: Create and validate signatures and certificate chains, and operate a PKI.",
   "LO6: Explain the TLS 1.3 handshake and configure and test TLS securely.",
   "LO7: Recognise and prevent implementation misuse, and select appropriate libraries.",
   "LO8: Build, test and document a secure file encryption and integrity tool."
  ],
