  "rating": null,
  "enrolled_count": 0,
  "card_variant": "random",
  "short_desc": "Organisations are embedding large language models (LLMs) into customer service, search, coding and operations. These systems bring new failure modes: models follow instructions hidden in the content they read, reveal data they were given, produce output that downstream systems trust too much, and take actions through tools with more authority than intended.",
  "lead_author": {
   "name": "RedKross Academy Faculty",
   "title": "AI Security",
   "role": "RedKross Research Foundation",
   "avatar": "/assets/crest-clean-circle.png"
  },
  "overview": "Organisations are embedding large language models (LLMs) into customer service, search, coding and operations. These systems bring new failure modes: models follow instructions hidden in the content they read, reveal data they were given, produce output that downstream systems trust too much, and take actions through tools with more authority than intended. This course teaches AI security from the defender's side: the AI system lifecycle and threat modelling; how LLM applications work (tokens, context, retrieval-augmented generation, tools and agents); prompt injection and layered mitigations; data and privacy risks; output handling and agency limits; model and data supply chain; safe, authorised adversarial evaluation (\"AI red teaming\") and risk reporting; and a capstone assessment of a controlled AI application. It maps throughout to the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications 2025, the NIST AI Risk Management Framework and its Generative AI Profile, and NIST's adversarial machine learning taxonomy.",
  "why_it_matters": [
   "Real incidents and cases already show the risks: a caching-library bug that exposed other users' conversation titles and some billing details in a popular chatbot service (2023); a European data protection authority temporarily limiting a chatbot's processing of national users' data (2023); indirect prompt injection reported against a workplace AI assistant (2024); a tribunal holding an airline responsible for what its website chatbot told a customer (2024); and malicious models on a public model hub that executed code when loaded (2024). Each is examined with its best available source."
  ],
  "intended_audience": [
   "Security engineers and architects, application security and AppSec champions working with AI features, SOC and IR staff, ML/AI engineers who need security depth, and risk and governance staff. Recommended after Courses 06, 12 and 17."
  ],
  "prerequisites": [
   "Course 01",
   "Course 04 (Python)",
   "Course 06 (web application security)",
   "Course 12 (secure SDLC and threat modelling)",
   "Course 17 (risk and governance) helpful"
  ],
  "required_software_hardware": [
   "Python 3.10+; Ollama with a small local model (tested with qwen3:4b, about 2.5 GB); 16 GB RAM recommended; no internet access is needed after the model download. A GPU is optional."
  ],
  "what_you_will_learn": [
   "Describe the AI system lifecycle, assets and trust boundaries, and build an AI threat model.",
   "Explain LLM application architecture - tokens, context windows, RAG, tools and agents - and its security implications.",
   "Explain direct and indirect prompt injection and design layered mitigations.",
   "Identify and control sensitive data exposure in training, retrieval and outputs, and apply data governance.",
   "Secure output handling and bound agency with tool permission policies.",
   "Assess and control the model and data supply chain.",
   "Plan and run safe, authorised adversarial evaluations and report risk.",
   "Assess a controlled AI application end to end and document risks, evidence and mitigations."
  ],
