  "card_variant": "random",
  "short_desc": "Security architecture designs systems so that security properties emerge from structure rather than from heroic operations. Zero trust is the architectural approach that removes implicit trust based on network location: every access request is authenticated, authorised and evaluated against dynamic policy, close to the resource.",
  "lead_author": {
   "name": "RedKross Academy Faculty",
   "title": "Security Architecture",
   "role": "RedKross Research Foundation",
   "avatar": "/assets/crest-clean-circle.png"
  },
  "overview": "Security architecture designs systems so that security properties emerge from structure rather than from heroic operations. Zero trust is the architectural approach that removes implicit trust based on network location: every access request is authenticated, authorised and evaluated against dynamic policy, close to the resource. This capstone-level course covers architecture foundations (requirements, trust boundaries, views); identity as the control plane, including MFA, device trust and privileged access; zero trust principles and components as defined by NIST SP 800-207 and the CISA Zero Trust Maturity Model; network architecture with micro-segmentation and secure gateways; application and data architecture including classification and key custody; resilience and monitoring architecture; architecture assurance through threat modelling, design review and control validation; and a final reference architecture with an implementation roadmap. It draws together Courses 08, 11, 12, 15 and 17.",
  "why_it_matters": [
   "Recent major incidents trace back to architecture: a remote-access portal without MFA at Change Healthcare (2024), a legacy test tenant and OAuth application with excessive access at Microsoft (2023-24), VPN appliances whose integrity checks could be deceived (2024), and a legacy flat network at the British Library that allowed attackers wider access than a modern design would have (2023). Each is examined with its best available source."
  ],
  "intended_audience": [
   "Security architects and engineers, solution and enterprise architects, senior SOC/IR and cloud engineers moving into design roles, and CISOs needing architectural fluency. Recommended after Courses 08, 11, 12, 15 and 17 (or equivalent experience)."
  ],
  "prerequisites": [
   "Course 01",
   "Course 02 (networking)",
   "Course 11 (cloud)",
   "Course 12 (secure SDLC, threat modelling)",
   "Course 15 (cryptography, PKI, TLS)",
   "Course 17 (risk, frameworks). Python and Docker basics for labs"
  ],
  "required_software_hardware": [
   "Python 3.10+; Docker Desktop or Docker Engine (alpine:3.20 image); a diagramming tool or plain text; 8 GB RAM."
  ],
  "what_you_will_learn": [
   "Derive security requirements and trust boundaries and document architecture views.",
   "Design identity-centric access with strong authentication, device trust and privileged access controls.",
   "Apply zero trust tenets and design policy decision and enforcement points.",
   "Design segmented networks and secure, encrypted connectivity.",
   "Design secure services and data protection with classification and key custody.",
   "Architect central logging and resilient, incident-ready systems.",
   "Assure architectures through threat modelling, design review and control validation.",
   "Produce a zero trust reference architecture and phased roadmap."
  ],
